# Highly Improved Photocurrent Density and Efficiency of Perovskite Solar Cells via Inclined Fluorine Sputtering Process

## abstract
Increase in incident light and surface modification of the charge transport layer are powerful routes to achieve high‐performance efficiency of perovskite solar cells (PSCs) by improving the short‐circuit current density (JSC) and charge transport characteristics, respectively. However, few techniques are studied to reduce reflection loss and simultaneously improve the electrical performance of the electron transport layer (ETL). Herein, an inclined fluorine (F) sputtering process to fabricate high‐performance PSCs is proposed. The proposed process simultaneously implements the antireflection effect of F coating and the effect of F doping on a TiO2 ETL, which increases the amount of light transmitted into the PSC due to the extremely low refractive index (≈1.39) and drastically improves the electrical properties of TiO2. Consequently, the JSC of the F coating and doping perovskite solar cell (F‐PSC) increased from 25.05 to 26.01 mA cm−2, and the power conversion efficiency increased from 24.17% to 25.30%. The unencapsulated F‐PSC exhibits enhanced air stability after 900 h of exposure to ambient environment atmosphere (30% relative humidity, 25 °C under dark condition). The inclined F sputtering process in this study can become a universal method for PSCs from the development stage to commercialization in the future.

Increase in incident light and surface modification of the charge transport layer are powerful routes to achieve high-performance efficiency of perovskite solar cells (PSCs) by improving the short-circuit current density $U_{\mathsf{S C}})$ and charge transport characteristics, respectively. However, few techniques are studied to reduce reflection loss and simultaneously improve the electrical performance of the electron transport layer (ETL). Herein, an inclined fluorine (F) sputtering process to fabricate high-performance PSCs is proposed. The proposed process simultaneously implements the antireflection effect of F coating and the effect of F doping on a $\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ ETL, which increases the amount of light transmitted into the PSC due to the extremely low refractive index $_{(\approx1.39)}$ and drastically improves the electrical properties of $\boldsymbol{\mathsf{T i O}}_{2}$ . Consequently, the $J_{s c}$ of the F coating and doping perovskite solar cell (F-PSC) increased from 25.05 to $26.07\:\mathsf{m A c m}^{-2}$ , and the power conversion efficiency increased from $24.77\%$ to $25.30\%$ . The unencapsulated F-PSC exhibits enhanced air stability after $\mathfrak{s o o h}$ of exposure to ambient environment atmosphere $30\%$ relative humidity, $25^{\circ}C$ under dark condition). The inclined F sputtering process in this study can become a universal method for PSCs from the development stage to commercialization in the future.
## 1. Introduction
Owing to the extensive use of nonrenewable fossil fuels and the subsequent catastrophic destruction caused to the environment, the development of technologies for utilizing renewable and clean energy sources such as the solar energy has become urgent. Perovskite solar cells (PSCs) are rapidly emerging as one of the most promising photovoltaic technologies owing to their high power conversion efficiency (PCE), easy manufacturing protocol, and low-cost fabrication processing.[1–6] Researchers are increasingly focusing on the development of high-performance PSCs so that they can be successfully commercialized, and many strategies to increase the PCE of PSCs have been reported over the past decade.[7–12]
Jeong et al. used a pseudo-halide anion formate to suppress the defects on the surface of $\alpha$ FA-based lead triiodide $(\alpha{\mathrm{-FAPbI}}_{3})$ and increase the crystallinity, affording a high PCE of $25.6\%$ .[13] Kim et al. recently reported that lithium-doped mesoscopic $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ can improve the electrical properties of mesoporous $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ to afford a high current density of over $26\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ and PCE of $25.3\%$ .[14] In addition, Min et al. designed a defect-minimized interface between a $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ electron transport layer (ETL) and perovskite, achieving a PCE of $25.5\%$ (certified) that was attributable to the enhanced charge extraction and transport.[15] In another study, Kim et al. reported a (polyacrylic acid (PAA)–quantum dot (QD)– $S\mathrm{nO}_{2}$ $@$ compact$\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ $\ensuremath{\left(\mathrm{c}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\right)}$ ) structure in which PAA-QD- $\mathbf{SnO}_{2}$ was deposited on $\mathsf{c}{\cdot}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ . The light absorption in this structure was significantly improved with a perovskite layer, achieving a short-circuit current density $(J_{\mathrm{SC}})$ of $26.4\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ .[16] To date, strategies to fabricate high-performance PSCs have focused on perovskite materials, charge transport materials (to reduce carrier loss), and photon management (to improve $J_{\mathrm{SC}})$ .[17–20]
Fluorine (F), the most electronegative element (electronegativity $=4.0$ ), can significantly alter the energy level of materials; consequently, the performance and stability of PSCs can be improved using F.[21–25] Because of the small atomic radius of F ( $120~\mathrm{pm}$ ; van der Waals radius is $147\mathrm{pm}$ , which is close to that of the hydrogen atom), fluorination of materials does not affect molecular packing.[26,27] Moreover, F has low polarizability because of the strong interaction between the electrons and nucleus. Consequently, F-based polymers have low refractive indices and low dielectric constants.[22,28] Owing to the strong $\mathrm{C-F}$ bond, fluorinated compounds exhibit higher thermal and oxidative stabilities,[29–31] thereby rendering fluorinated materials suitable for application in PSCs. Zhu et al. used the 1,3-dimethyl-3-imidazolium hexafluorophosphate $\left(\mathrm{DMMPF}_{6}\right)$ ionic liquid (IL) containing F to modify the perovskite surface. The ${\mathrm{DMMPF}}_{6}$ IL easily passivated the perovskite surface through electrostatic interactions between $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ ions and ${\mathrm{DMMPF}}_{6}$ , affording a high efficiency of $23.3\%$ .[32] Zhu et al. reported that the doping of a $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL with ammonium fluoride $\mathrm{(NH}_{4}\mathrm{F)}$ improved the electrical properties of the ETL of the PSC. The $\mathrm{NH}_{4}\mathrm{F}$ -treated $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ exhibited a more uniform morphology, reduced carrier recombination, and improved electron extraction from the perovskite, resulting in a significant increase in the PCE from $18.6\%$ to $20.5\%$ .[33] Yoo et al. combined an antireflection coating (ARC) of $\mathrm{MgF}_{2}$ , a low-refractiveindex material, with a moth-eye structure to reduce reflection loss in the visible region. As a result, $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ of the PSC increased by $11.80\%$ and the PCE increased from $18.2\%$ to $20.5\%$ .[34] Overall, fluorination can significantly improve PSC performance by improving the surface morphology, charge transport properties, and optical properties. However, to date fluorination has yet to achieve high photovoltaic performance.
In this paper, we propose a method for improving $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ and PCE of PSCs by applying an ARC of F and harnessing the F doping effect on the ETL using an inclined F sputtering process at the same time. The F coating was fabricated by a sputtering process using perfluoroalkoxy alkane (PFA) targets with the lowest refractive index (1.34) among polymers. The F coating forms bonds at high F contents, such as ${\mathrm{C}}{\mathrm{-}}{\mathrm{F}}_{3}$ and $\mathrm{C}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{F}_{2}$ , which result in a low refractive index owing to the low polarizability of F atoms. The antireflection (AR) F coating can reduce reflection losses and increase the amount of light transmitted into the PSC, thereby leading to an increase in $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ . In addition, inclined dual sputtering was employed such that F could be doped into the backside of the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL. Owing to the high electronegativity and small atomic size of F, the electrical properties of the F-doped $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ $\left(\mathrm{F}{\cdot}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\right)$ ETL were significantly improved, increasing by $45\%$ compared to the initial value. Consequently, the $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ of the F coating and doping perovskite solar cell (F-PSC) increased from 25.05 to $26.01\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , and the PCE increased from $24.17\%$ to $25.30\%$ . Thus, the inclined F sputtering process is a concise but high-impact method to improve the efficiency of PSCs by simultaneously implementing AR and F doping effects on the ETL.
## 2. Results and Discussion
## 2.1. Antireflection Effect by F Coating
Figure 1 shows the schematic of the fabrication process and the chemical and optical properties of the AR F coatings. During the sputtering of carbon nanotube (CNT)-PFA composite targets, C and F atoms (or molecules) were randomly deposited on the substrate through decomposition and recombination processes (Figure 1a). Figure 1b shows the cross-sectional transmission electron microscopy (TEM) images of the F coating. The sputtered F coating with nanometer-scale thickness was deposited on the substrate. Elemental mapping confirmed the uniform distribution of F throughout the $\mathrm{~F~}$ coating. Atomic force microscopy (AFM) images (Figure S1, Supporting Information) indicated a smooth surface ( $\mathrm{'}R\mathrm{a}=0.521~\mathrm{nm}_{\mathrm{}}$ ) of the F coating. Figure  1c shows the F 1s and C 1s X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) profiles. PFA exhibits two peaks corresponding to the ${\mathrm{C}}{\mathrm{-}}{\mathrm{F}}_{3}$ $(294.0\ \mathrm{eV})$ and ${\mathrm{C}}{\mathrm{-}}{\mathrm{F}}_{2}$ $(292.0\ \mathrm{eV})$ bonds. The F coating exhibits overlapping peaks originating from the $\mathrm{C-F}$ $(290.0~\mathrm{eV})$ , $\mathrm{C-CF_{n}}$ $(287.4~\mathrm{eV})$ , and ${\mathrm{C-}}({\mathrm{C}},{\mathrm{H}})$ $(285.4~\mathrm{eV})$ bonds, in addition to the aforementioned peaks at 294.0 and $292.0\ \mathrm{eV}.$ Because of the low surface energies of ${\mathrm{C}}{\mathrm{-}}{\mathrm{F}}_{3}$ $(6~\mathrm{mN}~\mathrm{m}^{-1})$ and ${\mathrm{C}}{\mathrm{-}}{\mathrm{F}}_{2}$ $18.5\ \mathrm{mN\m^{-1}}$ ), the $\mathrm{~F~}$ coating has a water contact angle (WCA) of $108^{\circ}$ , similar to that of PFA, indicating a hydrophobic surface.[35] Additionally, as shown in the spectroscopic ellipsometry (SE) results (Figure 1d), the F coating has a refractive index of 1.39 at $550\mathrm{nm}$ and exhibits a low refractive index over a wide wavelength range.
Figure 1e and Table 1 show the optical properties of F-doped tin oxide (FTO), $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ coated on FTO $\left(\mathrm{FTO}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\right)$ , and the F coating deposited on the glass/ $\mathrm{\FTO/TiO}_{2}$ (F coating $\mathrm{\Delta/FTO/TiO_{2})}$ substrate, respectively. After $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ was coated on the FTO glass, the average transmittance in the visible region decreased significantly from $78.2\%$ to $72.6\%$ . This is because when light travels from glass to FTO and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}.$ which have high refractive indices, light is reflected multiple times at the interface due to internal reflection, thereby decreasing the total amount of transmitted light. As a result, a reduced amount of incident light reaches the perovskite layer. However, after the AR F coating was applied, the average transmittance drastically increased by $3.2\%$ (from $72.6\%$ to $75.8\%$ at the optimized thickness.
According to the Fresnel equations, when the F coating was deposited on the glass substrate, the reflection coefficient was 0.027 for the light traveling from air $(n=1)$ to the F coating $(n\approx1.39)$ . This is an $\approx36\%$ decrease in the reflection coefficient of 0.042 that occurs when light is incident directly from air $(n=1)$ to glass $(n\approx1.52)$ . Thus, the reflection decreased owing to refractive index mismatch. In conclusion, the F coating deposited on the glass surface significantly increased the amount of light incident through the glass, and accordingly, the amount of light passing through the film with a high refractive index increased. As shown in the inset of Figure 1e, the reflection from F coating/FTO/ $\mathrm{\DeltaTiO}_{2}$ was reduced in relation to that from $\mathrm{FTO}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , and the text could be seen more clearly beyond the substrate. In Figure S2 (Supporting Information), the average transmittance of the F coating/ $\mathrm{FTO}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ substrate as a function of the $\mathrm{~F~}$ coating thickness increased from $72.6\%$ to $74.6\%$ in the visible range, and the average reflectance decreased from $6.6\%$ to $5.0\%$ under all thickness values. The average reflectance decreased by $24\%$ compared to that of $\mathrm{FTO}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ .
Figure $\mathrm{1f}$ shows the average transmittance from the FTO glass, $\mathrm{FTO}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , and $\mathrm{~F~}$ coating/ $\mathrm{{'FTO/TiO_{2}}}$ in the visible range; the figure of merit (FoM) values are summarized in Table  1. The FoM is an index for evaluating the performance of transparent conductive electrodes that are used in PSCs, display devices, and other similar applications. In the present study, Haacke’s FoM was calculated from the measured electrical and optical parameters using Equation $1.^{[36-38]}$
$$
\mathrm{FoM}=\frac{T_{\mathrm{avg}}^{10}}{R_{\mathrm{sh}}}
$$
where $T_{\mathrm{avg}}$ is the average transmittance obtained in the visible wavelength range of $400{-}800\ \mathrm{nm}$ and $R_{\mathrm{sh}}$ is the sheet resistance.
When $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ was coated on the FTO glass, the FoM value of the substrate decreased drastically from $12.2\times10^{-3}$ to $5.8\times10^{-3}\Omega^{-1}$ owing to the decrease in transmittance. When the F coating was deposited on this substrate, the sheet resistance remained unchanged $(7~\Omega~\mathrm{{sq}^{-1})}$ , while the transmittance increased owing to the antireflection effect. Thus, the FoM and average transmittance increased with a similar trend. For a $120\ \mathrm{nm}$ -thick F coating, the average transmittance increased the maximum in the visible range, with the highest FoM value of $9.0\times10^{-3}\Omega^{-1}$ . Thus, when the F coating with a low refractive index is applied to the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL coated on FTO, the FoM value and transmittance increase significantly.
## 2.2. F-Doped $\bar{\mathsf{T i O}}_{2}$ Electron Transporting Layer
Figure 2 shows the changes in the chemical and physical properties of the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL after F coating. The F coating was fabricated using an inclined dual-sputtering method, wherein power was applied simultaneously to the dual cathode using a mid-range frequency (MF) power supply, which is advantageous for high process efficiency and large-area coating. As shown in Figure 1a, the dual-sputtering cathodes were inclined at an angle of ${\approx}10^{\circ}$ to ensure uniform F coating and to ensure that the F plasma reaches both front and back sides of the substrate. The TEM image and elemental mapping (Figure  2a) confirmed the uniform distribution of $\mathrm{~F~}$ on $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ . F-doped $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ was labeled $\mathrm{F}{\cdot}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ (F coating thickness was $120~\mathrm{nm}$ , at which the AR effect was maximum). Time-of-flight secondary ion mass spectrometry (TOF-SIMS) (Figure 2b; Figure S3, Supporting Information) revealed a higher F content on the $\mathrm{F}{\cdot}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ surface than on the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ surface.
Next, the F 1s and O 1s XPS spectra (Figure 2c and Figure 2d) were acquired to investigate the change in the chemical structure of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{F}{\cdot}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ . No peak corresponding to F atom is observed for the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ spectrum (Figure  2c, bottom panel), while two peaks at binding energies of 689.2 and $685.8\ \mathrm{eV},$ corresponding to $\mathsf{F{-}C}$ and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2\cdot\mathrm{x}}{-\mathrm{F}_{\mathrm{x}}},$ respectively, are observed for $\mathrm{F{-}T i O}_{2}$ (Figure  2c,  top panel).[39] Figure S4 (Supporting Information) shows the survey spectra of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{F}{\cdot}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , and Table 2 lists the concentration of each atom (O 1s, Ti $2p$ , C 1s, and $\texttt{F}1s$ atoms). The atomic concentration of F 1s increases from 0 to $10.6\ \mathrm{at.}\%$ . Because F is more electronegative than oxygen $\mathrm{~F~}=4.0$ , $\mathrm{~O~}=3.44_{\cdot}$ ), the former can occupy an oxygen vacancy $\mathrm{(V_{O})}$ site in the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ structure [40] and form a $\mathrm{Ti-F}$ bond (bond energy $\mathbf{\tau}=581$ kJ $\mathrm{mol^{-1}}$ ), which is stronger than the $\mathrm{Ti}{-}\mathrm{O}$ bond (bond energy $=478\mathrm{~k~}$ J  mol−1).[41] Figure  2d shows the O 1s XPS spectra of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{F}{\cdot}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ . To investigate the $V_{\mathrm{{O}}}$ in the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ structure, the O 1s XPS spectra were fitted with three Gaussian curves centered at binding energies of 530.0, 531.4, and $532.4~\mathrm{eV},$ corresponding to lattice oxygen $\left(L_{\mathrm{O}}\right)$ , oxygen vacancy or defect $(V_{\mathrm{O}})$ , and chemisorbed oxygen species $(C_{0})$ , respectively. The low binding energy peak $(L_{0})$ originates from the lattice oxygen atoms $(\mathrm{O}^{-2})$ in a structure in which $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ is fully coordinated with $\mathrm{Ti}^{+4}$ ions, mainly in the bulk. The medium binding energy peak $(V_{\mathrm{O}})$ is attributed to the oxygen $(\mathrm{O}^{-},\mathrm{O}^{-2})$ vacancy of the metal oxide. The high binding energy peak $(C_{0})$ is attributed to organic contaminants on the surface, which can be significantly reduced by ultraviolet/ozone (UVO) treatment (Table S1 and Figure S5, Supporting Information).[42,43] When $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ was doped with F atoms, the peak area of $\mathrm{v_{o}}$ decreased from $12.0\%$ to $5.2\%$ (Table S1, Supporting Information). Therefore, the XPS results clearly confirm the reduction in $\mathrm{v_{o}}$ density of $\mathrm{F}{\cdot}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ due to the introduction of F atoms.
Figure 2e and Figure S6 (Supporting Information) show the X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{F}{\cdot}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ . The characteristic diffraction peak of the (101) plane of anatase $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ was observed at $26.84^{\circ}$ for both the samples.[44] Even after F doping, the peak did not shift, indicating that F effectively occupied the $V_{\mathrm{{O}}}$ sites. The average crystallite size of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , as calculated from the (101) diffraction peak of anatase using the Debye– Scherrer equation, also did not change after F doping (Table S2, Supporting Information). This is because the similar ionic radii of F (1.36  Å) and oxygen (1.40  Å) effectively reduce the lattice distortion in relation to that resulting from metal-cation doping.[40,45]
## 2.3. Enhanced Charge Transport Properties of the ${\mathsf{F}}{\cdot}{\mathsf{T i O}}_{2}$ Layer
Figure  3a shows the energy level diagrams of the perovskite devices. To analyze the effect of F doping on $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , ultraviolet photoemission spectroscopy (UPS) was conducted (Figure S7, Supporting Information). The Fermi level $(E_{\mathrm{F}})$ was calculated as $-(h\nu-E_{\mathrm{cut-off}})$ , where $h\nu_{}(=21.22\mathrm{~eV})$ is the energy of the ultraviolet photon source and $E_{\mathrm{cut-off}}$ is the secondary electron cutoff of the highest binding energy. The highest occupied molecular orbital (HOMO) energy was calculated from the cutoff value of the lowest binding energy. Figure S8 (Supporting Information) shows the absorption spectra and Tauc plots of pristine $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{F}{\cdot}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ deposited on the FTO/glass substrates. The bandgaps of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , as determined from the intersection of the baseline and dotted line in the Tauc plots, were same (3.57 eV) regardless of $\mathrm{~F~}$ doping. The lowest unoccupied molecular orbital (LUMO) energy was calculated from the difference between the HOMO energy and bandgap. According to the above results, $\mathrm{E_{F}}$ , HOMO energy and LUMO energy for pristine and $\mathrm{F}{\cdot}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ are shown in Table S3 (Supporting Information). The difference between $\mathrm{E_{F}}$ and the intrinsic energy level (Ei) is lower for $\mathrm{F}{\cdot}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ $(0.62\:\mathrm{eV})$ ) than for pristine $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ $(0.73\ \mathrm{eV})$ . This indicates that $\mathrm{F{-}T i O}_{2}$ exhibits more pronounced n-type characteristics than pristine $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ . To understand the nature of charge extraction between the ETLs and perovskite, we investigated the charge extraction barrier $(\Delta\mathrm{E_{e}})$ , which is the energy difference between the LUMOs of the ETLs and perovskite at the interface of the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/$ perovskite, respectively. The $\Delta E_{\mathrm{e}}$ of the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ /perovskite did not change after $\mathrm{~F~}$ doping (backside F coating). Interestingly, $\mathrm{F}{\cdot}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ had a smaller $\Delta E_{\mathrm{e}}$ at the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ interface $(0.09\ \mathrm{eV})$ than pristine $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ $(-0.12\ \mathrm{eV})$ . A smaller $\Delta E_{\mathrm{e}}$ indicates higher charge extraction efficiency. This implies that the charge extraction at the interface of $\mathrm{F}{\cdot}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/$ $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ /perovskite is more efficient than that at the interface of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/$ perovskite.[46] Moreover, the better match in LUMO energy between $\mathrm{F}{\cdot}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}.$ $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ , and perovskite allowed efficient charge transfer into $\mathrm{F}{\cdot}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ (Figure 3a).[47,48]
Figure  3b,c and Table  3 show the results of the Hall effect measurements on $\mathrm{F{-}T i O}_{2}$ as a function of the F-plasma time (F coating thickness parameters converted to F-plasma treatment time parameters). It is evident that the carrier concentration $(n_{\mathrm{bulk}})$ decreased remarkably when $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ was doped with F. The carrier concentration was primarily attributed to the $\mathrm{v_{o}}$ of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ . Two free electrons were generated by each $V_{\mathrm{O}}$ defect site according to the $V_{\mathrm{O}}$ reaction (Equation 2):
$$
{\mathrm O}_{\mathrm{o}}^{x}\to\frac{1}{2}{\cal O}_{2}\left(g\right)+\ddot{V}_{\mathrm{o}}+2e
$$
XPS analysis of the spectra in Figure  2c,d reveals that the generation of $V_{\mathrm{O}}$ in $\mathrm{F}{\cdot}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ was suppressed upon $\mathrm{~F~}$ doping, because of which the number of carriers also decreased. The carrier concentration and electron mobility $(\mu_{\mathrm{hall}})$ are related by Equation 3,
$$
\mu_{\mathrm{hall}}=\frac{1}{\rho e n_{\mathrm{bulk}}}
$$
where $\mu_{\mathrm{hall}}$ is the electron mobility, $\rho$ is the resistivity, and $e$ is the electronic charge $(1.6\times10^{-19}\mathrm{C})$ of the $\mathrm{F}{\cdot}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layer.
The electron mobility of $\mathrm{F}{\cdot}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ is inversely proportional to the carrier concentration. Figure  3c shows that the electron mobility of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ reached a maximum of $61.3~\mathrm{cm}^{2}~\mathrm{V}^{-1}~\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ at an F-plasma time of $37.5\ \mathrm{min}$ , corresponding to $45\%$ increase relative to the initial value $(42.2\thinspace\mathrm{cm}^{2}\thinspace\mathrm{V}^{-1}\thinspace\mathrm{s}^{-1})$ . The increased electron mobility enabled faster transport of the photogenerated electrons to the electrode (Regime I). When the F-plasma time was longer than $45~\mathrm{min}$ , the electron mobility gradually decreased because of the enhanced scattering with an increasing number of F atoms (Regime II).[49,50] The change in electron mobility, as shown in Figure  3d, can be explained as follows. The $V_{\mathrm{O}}$ present in conventional $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ limits the transport and collection of the photogenerated carriers. The initial increase in electron mobility in Regime I is attributed to the decrease in $V_{\mathrm{O}}$ , whereas the decrease in electron mobility in Regime II is a result of scattering by the excess F atoms. Figure S9 (Supporting Information) shows the scanning electron microscopy (SEM) and AFM images of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{F}{\cdot}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ . Although the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ surface has a rough surface, the $\mathrm{F{-}T i O}_{2}$ surface becomes smooth upon the incorporation of F atoms, which can reduce the contact resistance.[51] As a result, the electron trap sites are eliminated, and the recombination is low.[52]
Figure  3e shows the steady-state photoluminescence (PL) spectra of perovskites coated on different samples. Compared to the control sample, significant PL quenching was observed for the perovskite on $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{F}{\cdot}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , which was attributed to the higher electron mobility. Time-resolved photoluminescence (TR-PL) spectra were recorded to further study the carrier transport dynamics at the interface between the perovskite and $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and were fitted by a bi-exponential decay function (Figure 3f). The fast decay component $(\tau_{1})$ originates from the radiative recombination of free charge carriers in the bulk, while the slow decay component $(\tau_{2})$ is attributed to chargecarrier quenching at the interface. The fitted values are listed in Table S4 (Supporting Information). The PL decay of the perovskite on $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{F}{\cdot}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ was significantly faster than that on $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , suggesting that the electron transfer from the perovskite to the former $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{F}{\cdot}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ is more efficient than that to the latter.[46,53,54]
Indeed, the computational results using density functional theory calculations show that electron transfer can be improved as discussed below. Figure  3g shows the wavefunction of the LUMO overlaid on top of atomic structures of the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{F}{\cdot}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ anatase models, which indicates that F doping contributes to delocalization in the LUMO. In a surface model with a doping ratio of $12.5\%$ , LUMO is remarkably delocalized around F atoms, while this pattern is not clearly observed in the pure (101) surface. Noting that the electron population around the LUMO does not differ much between the two models (Figure S10, Supporting Information), it is possibly safe to conclude that F doping enhances the electron transport behavior in $\mathrm{F{-}T i O}_{2}$ .
## 2.4. Performance of F Coating and Doping Perovskite Solar Cells (F-PSC)
Conventional PSCs with a structure of glass/ $\mathrm{\FTO/TiO}_{2}/$ $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\vert$ /perovskite/octyl ammonium iodide (OAI)/N2,N2,N2,N2,N7′,N7′,N7′,N7-octakis(4-methoxyphenyl)-9,9- spirobi[9H-fluorene]-2,2,7,7-tetramine (Spiro-OMeTAD)/Au (Figure 4a) were fabricated. Figure 4b shows the cross-sectional
SEM image of the F-PSC. The F-plasma coating process was employed to improve the efficiency of the PSCs (Figure $4\upepsilon$ and Table  4). The bandgap of the perovskite is not changed after F coating (Figure S11, Supporting Information). In particular, $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ improved upon F coating and F doping (designated as the F-process). These processes enhanced the transmittance of glass/ $\mathrm{FTO}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and the electron mobility of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , thereby improving the photocurrent in the PSCs (Figure 1e; Figure S2, Supporting Information). F-PSC devices demonstrated significantly improved PCEs, with a maximum PCE of $25.30\%$ (reverse scan, $J_{\mathrm{SC}}=26.01~\mathrm{mA}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , open-circuit voltage $(V_{\mathrm{OC}})$ $=1.202\mathrm{V},$ and fill factor $(F F)=80.93\%)$ . This corresponds to an absolute PCE gain of ${\approx}1\%$ upon the F-process. The hysteresis indices were 0.050 and 0.056 for PSC and F-PSC, respectively (Figure S12 and Table S5, Supporting Information), indicating that the F-process did not affect hysteresis.
The enhanced photocurrent of the PSCs after the F-process was also verified by measuring the external quantum efficiency (EQE) of the PSCs with and without the F-process (Figure  4d). The EQE of the F-PSC increased over a broad range of visible light. To elucidate the enhanced photocurrent after the F-process, we compared the difference between the transmittances of the glass $\mathrm{{'FTO/TiO_{2}}}$ substrates (d(Transmittance)) and the difference between the EQEs of the control device and F-PSC (d(EQE)) (Figure 4e). Comparison reveals that the d(EQE) is proportional to d(Transmittance), indicating that the enhanced photocurrent results from the F-process, and this tends to increase for all F coating thicknesses (Figure S13, Supporting Information). As shown in the UV–vis absorption spectra (Figure S14, Supporting Information), light absorption intensity of the perovskite film with F-process was enhanced. The integrated photocurrents of the control device and F-PSC were calculated to be 24.37 and $25.23\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ , respectively, which are close to the $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ values obtained from the $J{-}V$ measurements. The same results were obtained when devices with different thicknesses of the F coating were considered (Figure S15a—b, Supporting Information). The reproducibility was evaluated by statistical analysis. The photovoltaic parameters of 20 corresponding devices indicated that the performance of the F-PSC was more reproducible compared to that of the control device (Figure 4f). Figure S15c—f (Supporting Information) shows the reproducibility of the F-PSC with different F coating thicknesses.
The PCE and photocurrent output at the maximum power point (MPP) of the devices (initial $\mathrm{PCE}_{\mathrm{Control}}=23.04\%$ $\mathrm{PCE_{F}}$ - PSC  = 24.10%) were continuously measured under simulated standard sunlight (AM 1.5G) (Figure $\ensuremath{4\mathrm{g}})$ . The PCE of the control devices and F-PSCs finally stabilized at 22.30 and $23.68\%$ , and the photocurrent density at 22.87 and $24.12\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ , respectively. We studied the long-term stability of the PSCs at $30\%$ RH for $900\mathrm{~h~}$ without encapsulation. The PCE of the F-PSC is stable at ${\approx}89\%$ of its original value while the control device shows ${\approx}83\%$ of its original PCE (Figure  4h). The enhanced environmental stability can be attributed to the hydrophobicity of the F-PSC compared to the control, which could be verified by WCA measurement of the F coating surface (Figure S16, Supporting Information).[55] In Figure 4i, we measured the stability of the control device and the F-PSC under full-spectrum light $(100\mathrm{mW}\mathrm{cm}^{-2})$ ) in $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ atmosphere. The PCE of the control device retains ${\approx}80\%$ after $350\mathrm{~h~}$ while the PCE of the F-PSC retains ${\approx}91\%$ of its initial value for $650\mathrm{~h~}$ . Thus, the result elucidates that the $\mathrm{F}{\cdot}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ has a positive effect on the long-term stability.
## 3. Conclusion
In conclusion, we have demonstrated that inclined F sputtering can implement an AR effect by virtue of F coating and F doping on the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL in PSCs. In this sputtering process, the average transmittance of the F-coated $\mathrm{FTO}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ increases significantly because of the very low refractive index of the F coating. Depositing an AR F coating is an effective method for reducing the up to $39.4\%$ reflection loss caused by FTO and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ . In addition, the inclined sputtering method allows $\mathrm{~F~}$ to be doped on the backside of the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ surface, which effectively suppresses oxygen vacancies in $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and enhances electron mobility. Under the combined effects of F coating and F doping, $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ of the F-PSC increases from 25.05 to $26.01\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , and the PCE increases from $24.17\%$ to $25.30\%$ . Regarding stability, the F-PSC exhibits enhancement in ambient air, retaining $590\%$ of its initial PCE after $900\mathrm{~h~}$ under $30\%$ RH condition. Likewise, the F-PSC exhibits a significant improvement in light stability under one-sun illumination in $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ atmosphere, with ${\approx}91\%$ of its initial efficiency retained after $650\mathrm{~h~}$ . This work demonstrates that the inclined F sputtering technology is a simple and effective method to increase $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ of PSCs through the improved transmittance of $\mathrm{FTO}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and electrical characteristics of the ETLs.
