## 4. Experimental Section
Fabrication of Sputtering Target and the F-Plasma Process: The CNT−PFA composite targets for sputtering were cut into disks with a diameter and thickness of $70.76~\mathsf{c m}$ and $6~\mathsf{m m}$ , respectively.[55] FTO glass (thickness: $650\ \mathsf{n m}$ , $15\ \mathsf{m m}\times15\ \mathsf{m m}$ , 2.2T, TEC7, Pilkington) was cleaned ultrasonically in deionized water, acetone, and 2-propanol for 10  min each. To fabricate the $\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ layer, $60~\mathsf{m L}$ of a titanium diisopropoxide bis(acetylacetonate)/ethanol (1:10) solution was sprayed using a spray gun at $450^{\circ}\mathsf C$ under an oxygen atmosphere. After the $\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ layer (thickness: $20\ n m$ ) was fabricated, the substrates were stored at $450~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $\rceil\boldsymbol{\mathsf{h}}$ . The F coating on FTO glass/ $\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ was fabricated using a power source with an MF (PE II, Advanced Energy) of $40~\mathsf{k H z}$ and a sputtering power density of $\mathsf{l}.85\ \mathsf{W}\mathsf{c m}^{-2}$ . The sputtering chamber was evacuated to a high vacuum level of $3.0\times10^{-6}$ Torr using a mechanical rotary pump and cryopump. The working pressure of argon gas was 8 mTorr, which was maintained using a mass flow controller. The targetto-substrate distance was maintained at $240~\mathsf{m m}$ .[56] To ensure a uniform F coating, the cathode head was inclined by ${\approx}70^{\circ}$ . The thicknesses of the F coatings were measured using a profilometer ( $\alpha\mathrm{\Omega}$ step 250, Tencor). F coatings with thicknesses of 90, 120, 150, 180, and $270\ n m$ were deposited by adjusting the coating time to 22.5, 30, 37.5, 45, and $52.5\mathrm{\min}$ , respectively.
Characterization of F Coating and $F_{-}T i O_{2}$ ETL: The refractive indices of FTO, $\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ , and the F coating were determined by SE (Elli-SE, Ellipso Technology; Figure S17, Supporting Information). After storing the complex refractive indices of the materials in the Essential Macleod program software (Thin Film Center), the optical properties were simulated according to the wavelength (Table S6, Supporting Information). Optical transmittance and reflectance were measured in the wavelength range of $300{-}1200~\mathsf{n m}$ using an optical spectrometer (U-4100, Hitachi). The sheet resistances of the samples were measured at room temperature using a standard four-point probe technique (MCPT610, Mitsubishi Chemical Analytech). The structure and elemental composition of the layers were investigated by field-emission TEM (JEMARM200F, JEOL). The specimens were prepared using a focused ion beam. The surface morphology was investigated by field-emission SEM (SEM, MAIA3, TESCAN) and AFM (XE-100, Park Systems). The AFM instrument was operated in the tapping mode with a scan range of $2\upmu\mathrm{m}$ $\times2\upmu\mathrm{m}$ .
The chemical structure was determined by XPS (AXIS NOVA, Kratos). The XPS measurement vacuum degree was $10^{-9}$ Torr, and the characteristic X-ray was Al-Kα (1486.6  eV). X-rays were generated by applying an electric field with an acceleration voltage of $75\mathrm{~\keV}$ and acceleration current of $70~\mathsf{m A}$ Depth profiling was performed by TOFSIMS (ION-TOF M6, Germany) using a sputtering beam of ${{02^{+}}}$ at $\sf{1}\ k e v$ with a raster size of $500~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}\times500~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ and an analysis beam of $\mathsf{B i}^{3+}$ at $30\mathrm{\keV}$ with a raster size of $100~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}\times100~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ . High-resolution XRD patterns were obtained using a high-power diffractometer (D/ MAX2500V/PC, Rigaku) at a potential of $40~\mathsf{k V}$ and current of $200~\mathsf{m A}$ with a Cu-rotating anode and $\mathsf{C u}\ \mathsf{K}\alpha$ radiation $(\lambda=0.7542\ \mathsf{n m}$ ) using a graphite monochromator and scintillation counter. The WCA was measured using a contact angle tester (Phoenix 300 Touch, Surface Electro Optics) with $5\upmu\upiota$ of deionized (DI) water.
The UPS measurements were conducted using ESCALAB 250XI. The electrical properties were measured using a Hall effect measurement system (HMS-7000, Ecopia) at a magnetic field of $0.543\top$ and operating current of $0.001\ \mathsf{m A}.$ The UV–vis−near-infrared absorption spectra were obtained on a Cary 5000 (Agilent) spectrophotometer, and the PL spectra were obtained on an nF900 instrument (Edinburgh Photonics) using a xenon lamp as the excitation source. The TR-PL measurements
