To investigate the electronic structures of the $\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ anatase surface and the effect of F doping, the projector-augmented wave (PAW) method implemented in the Vienna ab initio Simulation Package (VASP) was adopted.[57] Starting with the pure anatase $\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ structure with lattice parameters $\mathsf{a}=3.\mathsf{\bar{8}}02\mathrm{~\AA~}$ and $\mathsf{c}=9.748\mathrm{^{\prime}}$ , the $\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ (101) surface was simulated using a $(2\times3)$ periodic slab model (108 atoms) separated by a $75\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ thick vacuum region. To determine the surface doping effect, one surface O atom was replaced by an F atom, analogous to the F-doping ratio of $12.5\%$ . Geometry optimization of the surface models was conducted with a convergence criterion of $10^{-6}\mathsf{e V}\mathring{\mathsf{A}}^{-1}$ , where the top two layers were fully relaxed, while the bottom layer was fixed to represent the bulk region. A Monkhorst-Pack mesh of $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{\sf~k~}}$ -points $(2\times2\times1$ points) was used to sample the Brillouin zone with a cutoff energy of $520\mathrm{eV}$ and self-consistent energy convergence tolerance of $10^{-6}$ eV. To accurately predict the electronic structures of the transition metal oxides,[58] the Heyd-Scuseria-Ernzerhof (HSE06)[59,60] hybrid density functional was adopted.
Fabrication of F-PSC: $\mathsf{S n O}_{2}$ colloidal solution was purchased from Alfa Aesar. Titanium diisopropoxide bis(acetylacetonate), ethylenediaminetetraacetic acid (EDTA, $99\%$ ), $N,N$ -dimethylformamide (DMF, anhydrous, $99.8\%$ , dimethyl sulfoxide (DMSO, $99.9\%$ , cesium iodide (CsI, $99.9\%$ , methylamine hydrochloride (MACl, $98\%$ , 2-propanol (IPA, anhydrous, $99.5\%)$ , chlorobenzene (CB anhydrous, $99.8\%$ , acetonitrile (ACN, $99.8\%$ , 4-tert-butylpyridine (tBP), and Li-bis(trifluoromethanesulfonyl)imide (Li-TFSI, $99.95\%$ ) were purchased from Sigma–Aldrich. FK209 Co(III)-TFSI salt was purchased from Lumtec. Formamidinium iodide (FAI, $99.99\%$ , methylammonium bromide (MABr, $99.99\%\right)$ , and octylammonium iodide (OAI, $99\%$ ) were purchased from GreatCell Solar. Ethyl ether (DEE $99.5\%$ was purchased from Samchun. Lead iodide $(\mathsf{P b l}_{2},\ 99.99\%)$ and lead bromide ( $(\mathsf{P b B r}_{2},$ $98\%$ were purchased from TCI.
To fabricate a thin EDTA- $\mathsf{S n O}_{2}$ layer, $\mathsf{S n O}_{2}$ (from Alfa Aesar) was diluted 20 folds with DI water and mixed with EDTA $(0.2\mathrm{\mg\mL^{-1}})$ under overnight stirring at $80~^{\circ}C$ .[46] The $\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ -coated substrates were subjected to UVO treatment for $30\mathrm{\min}$ . The resulting solution was spin-coated onto the pretreated $\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ layer at 4000 rpm for $30~\mathsf{s}$ . The substrates were then heated to $150^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $20~\mathrm{min}$ .
The perovskite precursor solution was prepared by dissolving $245~\mathsf{m g}$ FAI, $1.29~\mathsf{m g}$ MABr, $75.6~\mathsf{m g}$ CsI, $748~\mathrm{~m~g~}\ \mathsf{P b l}_{2}$ , $4.40~\mathsf{m g}$ $\mathsf{P b B r}_{2}$ and $35.4~\mathrm{mg}$ MACl in a 1  mL DMF/DMSO (8:1) solution. The perovskite solution was filtered through a PTFE filter $(0.2~\upmu\mathrm{m})$ . After the UVO treatment, the filtered perovskite precursor solution was spincoated in sequential steps. The first step was 1000  rpm for 10  s with an acceleration of 2000 rpm∙s. The second step was 4000 rpm for $35\mathrm{~s~}$ with an acceleration of 1500  rpm∙s. During the second spin-coating step, 1 ml of diethyl ether was added dropwise onto the substrates $20~\mathsf{s}$ before the completion of spin coating. The perovskite films were then transferred onto a hotplate and annealed at $100~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $60~\mathrm{{min}}$ . After cooling the substrates, $15~{\mathsf{m M}}$ of OAI solution dissolved in 2-propanol was spin-coated on the perovskite layer at 3000  rpm for $30~\mathsf{s}$ . For the hole-transport layer, the spiro-OMeTAD solution was spin-coated onto the perovskite layer at 3000 rpm for $30~\mathsf{s}$ . Spiro-OMeTAD $(90~\mathrm{mg/mL}$ in CB) was doped with $39\upmu\up L$ tBP, $23\upmu\upiota$ Li-TFSI $(520\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ in ACN), and $70~\upmu\upiota$ Co-TFSI $(300~\mathrm{mg~mL^{-1}}$ in ACN). The devices were pumped under vacuum $(<70^{-6}$ Torr), and an $80~\mathsf{n m}$ -thick Au electrode was deposited by thermal evaporation. Based on the deposited $\mathsf{A u}$ electrode area, the active area of the device was $\mathsf{10}.5\mathsf{m m}^{2}$ .
Characterization of Device Performances and Stability: The solar cell performance was determined using a Keithley 2635A source measurement. A high-quality optical fiber was used to guide the light from a solar simulator to the device under AM 1.5G illumination at $100m\aleph\ c m^{-2}$ . The $J-V$ curves were obtained with a scanning rate of voltage interval $20~\mathsf{m V}$ and delay time $40~\mathsf{m s}$ from 1.30 to -0.01  V (reverse) or from $-0.01$ to $\ensuremath{\mathsf{1.30~v}}$ (forward). EQEs of the PSCs were measured using a QEX7 system (PV Measurement, Inc.).
The air stability measurements of the unencapsulated control devices and F-PSCs were performed at $30\%$ RH and $25^{\circ}C$ under dark condition. The PCEs of the devices were periodically obtained under AM 1.5G simulated sunlight illumination in an $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ glove box. The light stability of the devices was measured under a full-spectrum Xenon lamp in ${\sf N}_{2}$ atmosphere. A UV filter was not used during the aging process. For $J{-}V$ characterization, the devices were taken out of the aging chamber, cooled, and tested.
